title: When metadata is extracted
tags: [[Embedded metadata]]
kind: reference
summary: A reconcile phase, on by default, that reads each file once — the mfr_meta_extracted marker — so re-running never re-parses; clear the marker to read again.

Extraction is a phase of every reconcile, full and scoped, on by default (`metadata:
false`, `mf reconcile --no-metadata` to skip it) — like the MIME type.

A file may legitimately carry no embedded metadata (a `.txt`, a data blob), so "is an
`mfr_meta_*` field absent?" cannot decide whether to read it again — such files would be
re-parsed at every reconcile. Instead `mfr_meta_extracted = true` records that a file has
been analysed: the phase skips files that have it, and sets it (with whatever was found)
on the ones it reads. Re-running reconcile therefore neither re-parses nor grows the log.

A content change is not reflected until the marker — and, to replace them, the stale
`mfr_meta_*` values — are cleared; the same "clear to recompute" contract as `mfr_mime`
([[File metadata fields]]):

```
mf -n music metarecord -q 'mfr_path ->* "/music/retagged"' field unset mfr_meta_extracted -f
mf -n music reconcile --metarecord <uuid of /music/retagged>
```
